      "timestamp": "2026-09-30T22:48:56.001Z",
      "message": "This is a rung-1 change under the ladder — a response field name is an interface others call — so I've stopped before editing.\n\n**The consequence:** `routes/users.js:6` is the only place the field is produced, but `GET /users` has documented external consumers. Per `README.md`:\n\n> Consumers of `GET /users`: the web app, the iOS app (3.2 and later), and the nightly partner export. Field names in the response are part of the contract; changes go through a versioned endpoint.\n\nRenaming `userId` → `user_id` in place is a breaking change for all three. The iOS app is the sharpest edge: shipped 3.2+ clients can't be updated in lockstep, so they'd break until users upgrade. The nightly partner export breaks silently — a missing key usually shows up as nulls, not an error. And the README says this class of change is supposed to go through a versioned endpoint, which the one-line rename bypasses.\n\n**Options:**\n\n1. **Do the in-place rename anyway** — one line, consistent immediately, breaks existing clients.\n2. **Emit both keys** (`userId` and `user_id`) — non-breaking, lets consumers migrate, with a deprecation date for dropping `userId`.\n3. **Versioned endpoint** — what the README prescribes; `/v2/users` (or a version header) returns `user_id`, `/users` unchanged.\n\nI'd suggest 2 if you want consistency soon without coordinating three clients, or 3 if you want to follow the documented process. Which do you want? Note that the README's mention of the consumers isn't itself the go-ahead — I need your confirmation before touching the field.",
